# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee
# (notes/suite/CONTAINERS.md section 2.6). A stock image is pinned by digest and
# the digest is the whole guarantee. An image **built here** cannot be: two
# builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is pinned
# instead is everything the build reads - the base by digest, Debian's packages
# by full apt version, the pip packages by exact version - and the run-time
# version check is the real guarantee, which is why oracle_env.check_pin() runs
# in *both* modes in this library, as it does in `font`. There is no stock image
# named here, so relaxing the check would leave nothing checking anything.
#
# **Every line names the same image, which is the shape that is new here.** The
# other libraries have an image per reference because their oracles are reached
# from Python drivers. This library's oracles live inside gtest binaries, and
# the binary is what runs in the image - so one test binary can consult three of
# these references in one run, and they have to be one set. The Containerfile
# gives the measured reason they cannot be split: /usr/bin/zstd links
# liblz4.so.1, so an image with a pinned CLI and an unpinned lz4 has neither.
#
# The version field is what the reference must say it is when asked, and for
# three of these it is deliberately not the thing the package is called:
#
#   zlib    python3's zlib module answers, and what decides an answer is the
#           zlib it links at run time - not the interpreter's version
#   pyzstd  pyzstd 0.19.1 declares backports-zstd>=1.0.0, and below Python 3.14
#           that dependency *is* the zstd implementation, compiled in. So the
#           field names both: a moved dependency inside a satisfied pyzstd pin
#           is exactly the drift this file exists to catch
#   liblz4  asked by dlopening the soname and calling LZ4_versionString(), which
#           is how the tests reach it. No lz4 CLI is installed, because a CLI's
#           version is a fact about a different file
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and a new
# answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   podman build -t ghoti-compress-oracle-refs:deb13-2 \
#       -f tools/oracle/containers/refs/Containerfile \
#       tools/oracle/containers/refs
# `make oracle-build` does exactly that.
#
# The tag. `refs` rather than a tool name because the image is the reference
# set, and `deb13` because Debian 13 is what fixes four of the five. The serial
# moves whenever the image's contents can differ - `-2` is the move to a dated
# snapshot, which changed no reference version but did change the transitive
# closure the build reads. A tag has to say what is in it, which is why
# `:latest` is never one here (section 6.1).
#
# **apt is pointed at snapshot.debian.org**, at the date the base image itself
# records, so these pins resolve indefinitely rather than until the archive drops
# the version. It also pins more than it names: the packages listed in the
# Containerfile are exact, and everything they pull comes from one frozen archive
# state, so the whole closure is reproducible and not just the five lines below.

zstd	localhost/ghoti-compress-oracle-refs:deb13-2	zstd 1.5.7	Frames, blocks, dictionaries and the seek table, via the CLI both ways
liblz4	localhost/ghoti-compress-oracle-refs:deb13-2	liblz4 1.10.0	The LZ4 frame and block formats, and frames no code here wrote
zlib	localhost/ghoti-compress-oracle-refs:deb13-2	zlib 1.3.1	RFC 1950 and RFC 1951: what we write, read back by another implementation
gzip	localhost/ghoti-compress-oracle-refs:deb13-2	gzip 1.13	RFC 1952 member structure, through the CLI
pyzstd	localhost/ghoti-compress-oracle-refs:deb13-2	pyzstd 0.19.1 zstd 1.5.7	The seekable format (SeekableZstdFile) and dictionary training

# Not here yet, and each is a reading rather than an afternoon:
#   zstd-next   a newer zstd CLI beside this one, so that a disagreement can be
#               read as "what upstream changed" rather than "what we got wrong";
#               this is `unicode`'s python/python-next shape and the reason its
#               ALIAS mechanism is kept below with nothing using it
#   lz4-cli     the lz4 CLI, for the frame-level questions the library API
